@* One inline SVG from the site's own icon family, drawn to Fluent 2's icon metrics: a 24x24 grid
   with a 20x20 live area, a single 1.5px stroke weight, round caps and round joins, and no fill.
   The strokes are `currentColor`, so an icon takes the color of the text it sits in and follows the
   theme without a second asset - which is the whole reason the emoji this replaced had to go: a
   glyph rendered by the OS font cannot be re-coloured, cannot be sized against the type ramp, and
   is a different picture on every platform.

   Every icon in the family is drawn to those metrics, so the `<svg>` carries them once here rather
   than per path. A mark that cannot be drawn as a stroke - a filled brand logo, say - does not
   belong in this dictionary: it would need its own `fill`, and there is no path for one.

   The markup is a compile-time constant looked up by name, so rendering it as a MarkupString
   inserts nothing a caller could influence. An unknown name renders nothing rather than throwing:
   a missing icon should not be able to take a documentation page down with it.

   HtmlToMarkdownService skips `svg` outright, so none of this reaches an MCP client - a decorative
   icon is exactly the markup that conversion exists to strip. *@

@if (_paths.TryGetValue(Name, out var path))
{
    <svg class="@Class"
         width="@Size" height="@Size" viewBox="0 0 24 24"
         fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"
         stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
         aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">@((MarkupString)path)</svg>
}

